# `actuarial::losses::compound_negbin`

Mean and variance of a compound negative binomial aggregate loss.

## Signature

```cpp
actuarial::losses::AggregateMoments compound_negbin(double r, double p, double severity_mean, double severity_variance, pluss::Platform platform = pluss::Platform::CPU);
```

## What It Does

The aggregate of a negative binomial number of claims ([`negbin_moments`](negbin_moments.md)) with a severity of mean {math}`\mu_X` and variance {math}`\sigma_X^2`: {math}`E[S]=E[N]\mu_X` and {math}`\mathrm{Var}[S]=E[N]\sigma_X^2+\mathrm{Var}[N]\mu_X^2`. The count variance enters through the squared mean claim, so an overdispersed count raises the aggregate variance well above the Poisson figure at the same mean. It is the one-call form of [`negbin_moments`](negbin_moments.md) followed by [`aggregate_moments`](aggregate_moments.md).

## Parameters

`r`, `p`
: The negative binomial parameters: `r` greater than 0 and `p` strictly between 0 and 1.

`severity_mean`, `severity_variance`
: Mean and variance of one claim; finite and 0 or more.

`platform`
: Where the result is placed. Default `pluss::Platform::CPU`.

## Returns

An [`AggregateMoments`](AggregateMoments.md) of two rank-zero series.

## Errors

Throws the `negbin_moments` errors for `r` and `p`, and `compound_negbin: severity_mean must be >= 0` and `compound_negbin: severity_variance must be >= 0`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: a portfolio

Mean count 4.5, severity mean 100 and variance 10,000.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto m = actuarial::losses::compound_negbin(3.0, 0.4, 100.0, 10000.0);
  out.writeln("mean =", m.mean.item<double>(), " variance =", m.variance.item<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: by hand

E[N] = 4.5 and Var[N] = 11.25.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const auto m = actuarial::losses::compound_negbin(3.0, 0.4, 100.0, 10000.0);
  out.writeln(m.mean.item<double>(), 4.5 * 100.0);
  out.writeln(m.variance.item<double>(), 4.5 * 10000.0 + 11.25 * 100.0 * 100.0);
}
```

### Example 3: against Poisson at the same mean

The overdispersed count gives a larger aggregate variance.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const auto nb = actuarial::losses::compound_negbin(3.0, 0.4, 100.0, 10000.0);
  const auto po = actuarial::losses::compound_poisson(4.5, 100.0, 10000.0);
  out.writeln("same mean:", nb.mean.item<double>(), po.mean.item<double>());
  out.writeln("negbin variance", nb.variance.item<double>(), " poisson variance", po.variance.item<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: the same as the two steps

Equal to aggregate_moments on the negative binomial moments.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const auto n = actuarial::losses::negbin_moments(3.0, 0.4);
  const auto a = actuarial::losses::aggregate_moments(n.mean, n.variance, pluss::make_series(100.0), pluss::make_series(10000.0));
  const auto b = actuarial::losses::compound_negbin(3.0, 0.4, 100.0, 10000.0);
  out.writeln(a.variance.item<double>(), b.variance.item<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: the errors

Invalid count parameters and severity moments are refused.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::losses::compound_negbin(0.0, 0.4, 100.0, 10000.0); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::losses::compound_negbin(3.0, 1.0, 100.0, 10000.0); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::losses::compound_negbin(3.0, 0.4, 100.0, -1.0); });
}
```

